            <ArticleTitle>Efficacy of therapeutic fluoroscopy-guided lumbar spine interventional procedures.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>649-56</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinimag.2013.02.006</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-7071(13)00056-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the benefit of fluoroscopy-guided lumbar spine interventional procedures in treatment of low back pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This prospective descriptive study was performed on 60 patients with back/radicular pain after showing no improvement with conservative treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One hundred and two injection sessions were done (average 1.7 injection per patient). Caudal and lumbar transforaminal injections were effective in 55.9% and 78.5%, respectively. Facet and sacroiliac interventions were effective in 28.3% and 10%, respectively. Complications occurred in 20% of the procedures.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lumbar injections improved pain/disability related to discogenic lumbar spinal diseases. Efficacy of facet and sacroiliac injections is limited.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
